 and he comes at her again just  the way he did moments before when he  brutally beat her and caused her serious  physical harm  the prosecution told the jury in their  opening the two veterans may have fought  but this case isn't about self-defense  this case is about a defendant who  brought a gun to a fist fight and  finished that fight by shooting the  father of her child  with one shot  one kill  this case is not a whodunit  okay  identity is not an issue when people  live together they may have unhealthy  relationships they may not be able to  live with each other and they may not be  able to live without each other  but at the end of the day  an unarmed man was shot  their fight was over  there was no reason  to pull that trigger  civil rights attorney philip hamilton  and co-host terry austin are back to  break down opening statements in ohio  the audrey cole terry how did the  prosecution push back on the argument of  self defense we kind of saw it there but  was it effective  i thought that the prosecution's  argument was very effective brian listen  the jury can relate to anyone who has a  marital argument because people argue  all the time and it doesn't need to end  up with someone being killed so they're  going to have to determine whether or  not this force was reasonable and i  think the burden of proof for the  prosecution might be hard the  prosecution is going to have to make  sure that each and every one of those  jurors thinks  beyond a reasonable doubt that you know  this was not necessary yeah philip i'm  guessing the defense attorney would say  something a little different but you  being a former public defender how do  you think the defense's opening was  in saying that um this was self-defense  and do you think this will lead to the  defendant testifying  i mean thematically it's masterful when  you're dealing with facts like these  it's the only route with which you can  go as a competent defense attorney so to  come out tell the story set